# Business and Platform Admin — Two-Module Implementation Plan

Reviewed and rewritten September 15, 2026 against the current repository.
Status: proposal; this review changes the plan only.

## 1. Objective and boundary

Keep one ServiceDesk.Web Angular application, one API deployment, and the existing
development ports. Give platform operators separate routes, presentation, and
navigation, supported by independent server authorization and database permissions.

Organize the SaaS into exactly two functional modules within this application:

| Module | Login page | Protected area | API namespace |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Business | /login | /app/* | /api/v1/businesses/{businessId} |
| Platform Admin | /platform-admin/login | /platform-admin/* except login | /api/v1/admin/* |

Business contains customer, job, catalog, scheduling, estimate, invoice, team,
subscription, and business settings workflows. Platform Admin contains operator
login, workspace administration, plans, backups, and platform audit workflows.
Each module owns its login presentation, shell, navigation, context, and permissions.
The admin login is a page within Platform Admin, not a third module or application.
Shared authentication callbacks and public document links remain shared routes.
These are logical feature modules; Angular standalone components and route trees
are sufficient, without introducing NgModule classes or additional deployments.

This is logical separation within a shared origin and deployment. Routes, themes,
lazy loading, and browser-storage flags do not isolate same-origin scripts or XSS.
Every API request must enforce its own authentication and authorization.

## 2. Findings requiring changes to the original proposal

| Current implementation or original proposal | Required correction |
| --- | --- |
| AuthService combines Auth0 and development identities. | Do not overwrite tenant userId or rely on a browser flag to establish platform identity. |
| Proposed guard trusts sessionStorage. | Validate current operator access with a server endpoint before rendering platform content. |
| PermissionAuthorizationHandler checks tenant membership, permission and scope claims, but not platform_role. | Add dedicated platform requirements and authorization handling; prevent generic tenant/claim fallback from granting platform privileges. |
| Proposed development header grants OperationsAdmin to any GUID. | Require an explicitly enabled, configured identity and active server-side administrator record. |
| TenantResolutionMiddleware treats every businessId route parameter as tenant scope, including admin business routes. | Classify tenant and platform endpoints explicitly; target business IDs on admin routes must not trigger tenant membership resolution. |
| PlatformAdminPage injects HttpClient and manually sends X-Dev-User-Id with a demo-owner fallback. | Move HTTP calls into a typed platform service; remove manual headers and fallback identities. |
| PlatformAdminController hard-codes the demo owner as mutation actor. | Resolve actor identity from validated platform context. |
| BaseDAL platform methods use the tenant connection string and do not explicitly clear tenant context. | Use separately configured restricted platform credentials through the shared BaseDAL mechanics and clear tenant context on acquisition. |
| The existing page eagerly loads all tabs and hides several failures as empty data. | Fetch authorized sections on demand and distinguish denied, empty, loading, error, and retry states. |

These are source-review findings. They do not establish that current platform APIs,
RLS behavior, or production authentication have passed runtime tests.

## 3. Platform identity and permission model

### Server-owned identity

Reuse configured OIDC/Auth0 authentication in production. Validate signature,
issuer, audience, and expiry, then map the external subject through the established
application-user mapping; do not assume OIDC subjects are GUIDs.

Resolve an active row in the existing platform.Administrators table. It already has
UserId, RoleCode, and IsActive, with Support, BillingAdmin, and OperationsAdmin roles
and a foreign key to auth.Users. Do not add a parallel role table or automatically
make tenant owners platform administrators.

Add an Application platform-context contract and administrator-resolver interface,
an Infrastructure implementation, and API integration. Context supplies the verified
operator ID, role, and effective permissions. Actor IDs never come from request bodies.
Recheck active administrator status for protected requests so revocation takes effect
after login; browser guard state is not continuing authorization.

### Dedicated policies

Register platform permission constants with a dedicated platform requirement/handler.
Tenant membership resolution and the generic permission/scope fallback must not satisfy
these requirements. Keep tenant permission enforcement independent.

Use this explicit proposed mapping:

| Capability / existing policy | Support | BillingAdmin | OperationsAdmin |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Workspace summaries: platform:Support | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| Plans and entitlements: platform:BillingAdmin | No | Yes | No |
| Metrics, status changes, backups/restores and audit: platform:OperationsAdmin | No | No | Yes |

OperationsAdmin is not implicitly a superuser. The current schema assigns one role
per user. Show only permitted sections and route the platform entry to the first
permitted section. A combined-role model would require an explicit schema/policy change.

Tenant subscription entitlements do not grant platform privileges. Tenant operations
retain their own membership, permission, and subscription checks.

### Current-operator contract

Add GET /api/v1/admin/me, requiring an authenticated, active platform operator.
Return a minimal response with operator ID, display name/email, role, and effective
permissions. Anonymous requests return 401; authenticated non-operators or inactive
operators return 403.

Implement its DTO, named access policy, Application query/handler, service method,
OpenAPI entry, and endpoint index together. Do not return tenant membership lists or
secrets. Frontend guards use this endpoint, not an isPlatformAdmin storage flag.

## 4. Development and production authentication

A dedicated X-Dev-Platform-Admin-Id header is acceptable only for local development:

1. Require both IsDevelopment() and an explicit disabled-by-default platform-demo setting.
2. Match the exact configured identity and resolve its active administrator record.
   A valid GUID alone must never grant a role.
3. Provision auth.Users and platform.Administrators with a development-only seed script.
   Do not add tenant membership for the operator.
4. Use centralized header constants. Reject conflicting identity mechanisms rather than
   making the platform header globally override tenant or Bearer identity.
5. Register/select authentication schemes explicitly in Program.cs, including behavior
   when Auth0 is configured. Outside Development, ignore development headers and fail
   closed when production authentication is unavailable.
6. Show the quick-login button only under explicit frontend development configuration.
   Backend enforcement is mandatory even if the button is hidden.

Production platform login uses the configured IdP flow, including the operator MFA
policy required by the deployment. Do not create a nonfunctional email/password form
or collect passwords while authentication is unconfigured. Display configuration status
instead. Missing OIDC configuration must not silently enable a production demo login.

Verify actual Bearer-interceptor registration: app.config.ts currently supplies an
Auth0 allowed list, but configuration alone does not prove tokens attach to requests.

## 5. Tenant middleware, database access, and audit

### Endpoint classification

Add explicit metadata for tenant-scoped and platform-scoped endpoints. Tenant middleware
must resolve membership only for tenant endpoints, not simply whenever businessId exists.
An admin target business ID is a resource selector, not tenant impersonation.

Do not bypass tenant resolution based on a user-controlled header or loose URL match.
Test admin business detail/status routes and ordinary tenant routes independently.

### Database boundary

Keep BaseDAL as the single shared implementation of database mechanics. Keep platform
feature SQL local to the PlatformAdminService methods that execute and map it.

Configure a separate restricted platform database principal/connection, available only
through platform infrastructure wiring. Missing platform configuration must fail closed;
it must not fall back to tenant credentials. Store credentials outside source control.
Neither runtime principal may be sa or db_owner. Tenant credentials must not acquire
platform write permissions.

Explicitly clear SESSION_CONTEXT(N'BusinessId') on every platform connection acquisition
and set the validated tenant ID on every tenant acquisition, including pooled reuse.

Null tenant context must continue to expose no tenant rows. Existing platform summaries
join app.Members and app.Subscriptions; validate those joins with the real RLS policies
before treating their counts as live. Do not fix empty counts by disabling RLS or granting
unrestricted tenant-table access. Design reviewed, narrowly scoped aggregate access
(such as signed database modules or maintained platform summaries), document its grants,
and test it with the platform principal before enabling cross-tenant metrics.

Platform membership does not imply tenant-data access. Any future support access must
use the existing tenant-approved, expiring, revocable read-only support grants.
Impersonation and automatic tenant membership are outside this change.

### Commands and audit

Application handlers own validation, permissions, state rules, and transaction boundaries.
Controllers bind and map results. Remove hard-coded actors from business status, plan
creation, and restore commands. Use validated platform context for every actor.

Write aggregate changes and append-only platform audit events atomically. Include
operator, target business where applicable, UTC time, action, trace ID, and safely
serialized metadata. Avoid logging secrets or constructing audit JSON by string
concatenation. Return safe ProblemDetails through central exception handling.

Require rowversion/ETag checks for updates and idempotency for queued/high-impact commands.
A real restore must run through an explicitly authorized background operation with
202 Accepted and a status URL, not directly inside an HTTP request. If the existing
restore control only records metadata, label it unavailable/prototype until execution
and status tracking exist. Verify restore behavior only in disposable environments.

## 6. Angular organization and session behavior

Keep the Business route tree in AppShell and lazy-load the Platform Admin feature
tree outside it. The module route structure is:

```text
Business module
  /login                         Public business login
  /app/*                         Protected business shell and pages

Platform Admin module
  /platform-admin/login          Public operator login
  /platform-admin                Protected entry; first permitted section
  /platform-admin/overview        Protected platform shell
  /platform-admin/workspaces
  /platform-admin/plans
  /platform-admin/backups
  /platform-admin/audit
```

Remove Platform admin from tenant navigation. Retire /app/admin through an explicit
redirect to the guarded platform entry; never render an unguarded legacy admin page.

Place the explicit login child before the protected empty-path shell route in the
platform route configuration. Apply platform access guards to that shell and its
protected children, not to the entire /platform-admin prefix: anonymous users must
be able to reach /platform-admin/login without a redirect loop. The login page renders
outside the authenticated platform shell. Use a full-path match for the base entry
redirect, and resolve its destination from the operator's permitted sections.

Wait for /api/v1/admin/me before rendering protected platform content.
Anonymous users go to /platform-admin/login; authenticated non-operators see access denied.
Network failures show retry rather than empty dashboards or login loops. Section
visibility and direct-route checks use the same effective permission mapping.
Do not introduce /platform-login as another canonical login route. If an earlier
implementation has exposed it, add a compatibility redirect to /platform-admin/login.
After IdP callback, validate any return path as a local route within the intended
module and recheck access before navigation.

Move platform-admin.page.ts under features/platform/. Reuse/split its section views
so route state controls selection, refresh, deep links, and browser Back. Do not
maintain independent sidebar and tab selection state. A move alone is insufficient:
remove its bizHeader, direct HttpClient calls, and unused WorkApiService import.

Add PlatformAdminApiService with typed request/response models and a separate
platform UI/context service. Fetch only authorized sections when visited.

Authentication handling must match approved API origins and exact path boundaries:
platform development identity only on /api/v1/admin and descendants; tenant identity
only on approved tenant/auth requests. Do not attach identity to third-party requests,
public endpoints, or all requests merely because platform mode is active. Strip
conflicting manual development headers. Verify production Bearer requests separately.

### Session transitions

Keep operator UI state in memory and revalidate after reload. Storage may preserve
navigation preferences but cannot establish operator authority. Do not replace the
tenant user ID during platform development login.

Production may share one IdP identity across both areas. Define two distinct actions:

- Exit platform clears platform UI/cache and returns to tenant login/entry. Tenant
  membership must be resolved normally. This is not IdP logout or privilege revocation.
- Sign out performs the configured authentication logout and clears this application's
  identity/context/cache keys. Do not use blanket localStorage.clear() or
  sessionStorage.clear().

Handle token expiry, another-tab logout, account switching, and stale tenant IDs.
A previous identity's cached data or privileges must not reappear after switching.
Concurrent different production identities on the same origin are outside this plan.

### Presentation

Use distinct slate/indigo branding, Platform Operations title, operator identity/role,
and permission-aware navigation. Remove the tenant workspace picker and tenant bottom
navigation from this shell. Provide an accessible mobile menu with keyboard/focus
handling at 390px, 768px, 1024px, and desktop.

Every remote section needs loading, empty, validation, error, retry, and success states.
Disable duplicate writes while active. Do not disguise denied requests as zero counts.
Show unavailable features as unavailable.

## 7. Implementation order and affected files

1. Define the Business and Platform Admin route trees, including their login pages,
   shells, platform identity, role mapping, /admin/me, endpoint classification, and
   authentication-mode contracts.
2. Implement server resolver/context/policies, development gates, and middleware
   classification; prove negative authorization cases.
3. Configure restricted platform SQL access through BaseDAL; validate RLS-safe summaries,
   actor attribution, transactions, concurrency, and audit requirements.
4. Add frontend platform API/context services, guards, /platform-admin/login, shell,
   and lazy routes with the public login outside the protected shell.
   Move/refactor existing views and remove manual headers and unauthorized eager loads.
5. Verify tenant and operator flows, update documentation, and run repository checks.

| Area | Main changes |
| --- | --- |
| Angular core | auth.service.ts, api-context.interceptor.ts; new platform API/context services and guard |
| Angular composition | app.config.ts, app.routes.ts, layout/app-shell.ts, features/platform/* |
| API security | Program.cs, development handler/defaults, dedicated platform policies/context integration |
| API endpoints | PlatformAdminController.cs, endpoint metadata, TenantResolutionMiddleware.cs |
| Application | PlatformContracts.cs, operator resolver/context interfaces, queries/commands/handlers, permissions |
| Infrastructure | PlatformAdminService.cs, administrator resolver, BaseDAL.cs, service registration |
| Database/configuration | Development seed, restricted platform principal/grants, reviewed RLS-safe summary access |
| Tests/documentation | API and SQL integration tests, Angular tests, docs/backend-spec OpenAPI/index, phase/status docs |

Angular paths above are relative to src/ServiceDesk.Web/src/app. Follow AGENTS.md for
inward dependencies, typed SQL parameters, cancellation, query placement, and formatting.

## 8. Acceptance criteria

| Scenario | Required result |
| --- | --- |
| Anonymous opens /platform-admin/login | Operator login renders without the protected shell or a redirect loop |
| Anonymous opens a protected /platform-admin/* page | Redirects to /platform-admin/login; no protected content renders |
| Business login and Admin login | Separate module entry pages in the same application and port |
| /app/admin legacy link | Redirects to the guarded platform entry; never renders inside the business shell |
| Tenant owner edits storage flags or directly calls admin API | No elevation; authenticated request receives 403 |
| Anonymous admin request | 401; no platform content |
| Unknown/forged demo GUID or conflicting headers | Rejected; no role issued |
| Demo headers outside Development or with demo mode off | No development authentication |
| Each role opens allowed and forbidden sections/APIs | Matches the explicit matrix, including direct URLs |
| Operator deactivated with page already open | Subsequent API requests denied; stale UI privileges cleared |
| Admin route includes target businessId | Platform authorization applies without tenant membership lookup |
| Operator uses tenant route without membership/support authorization | No tenant access |
| Mutation audit | Correct operator; atomic change/audit; stale versions and retries handled |
| Tenant/platform pooled connection reuse in both orders | No tenant-context leakage or accidental privilege expansion |
| Summary queries with real RLS | Accurate authorized aggregates, not misleading zero counts |
| Refresh, deep link, Back, expiry, switch account, logout | Consistent identity, route, cache, and permissions |
| Desktop/mobile and keyboard navigation | Usable menus, focus, forms, tables, errors, and retry |
| Existing tenant workflows | Continue under tenant permission and entitlement checks |

Run:

```powershell
dotnet format ServiceDesk.slnx --verify-no-changes
dotnet build ServiceDesk.slnx --no-restore
dotnet run --project tests/ServiceDesk.Api.Tests --no-build
npm --prefix src/ServiceDesk.Web run build
npm --prefix src/ServiceDesk.Web test -- --watch=false
```

Also test affected API routes against SQL Server using both restricted principals and
inspect the actual visible desktop/mobile browser session. Record HTTP outcomes,
permission denials, persistence/audit evidence, and blocked checks. Route headings or
rendered text length alone do not prove functional correctness or authorization.

Complete only when contracts, security, SQL behavior, actor attribution, session
transitions, UI, and documentation agree. Missing OIDC setup, unavailable SQL access,
or incomplete restore/summary implementations must be reported explicitly.
